course: myths
lesson: 4
title: Colds, coughs and the weather
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: "Coat ON, or you'll catch your death!" shouts Nan from the door, and half the country has heard it every winter for a hundred years: cold air, wet hair and rain give you a cold.
explain: It feels true, because colds really do come in winter. But a cold is caused by a virus, a tiny germ that has to come from another person: a sneeze on the bus, a cough in class, a hand on a door handle.
explain: Winter helps the viruses because we all crowd indoors with the windows shut, and some cold viruses last longer in cold, dry air; being chilly on your own, with nobody's germs about, does not give you a cold.
explain: So Nan's coat is still a good idea, because being freezing is miserable and in very cold weather getting soaked can be dangerous. She is right about the coat and wrong about the reason.
explain: Once you know the real cause, the real fixes make sense: wash your hands, catch sneezes in a tissue, rest, drink plenty, and keep antibiotics for germs they can actually kill.
fact: Colds are caused by viruses that spread from people, through coughs, sneezes and hands; being cold or having wet hair does not by itself give you a cold.
fact: Colds are more common in winter mostly because people are indoors close together, and some cold viruses survive better in cold, dry air.
fact: Wet hair or rain can make you feel cold, but they bring no viruses; in very cold weather, getting soaked can lead to dangerous chilling called hypothermia.
fact: "Feed a cold, starve a fever" is an old saying; doctors say with either one you should drink plenty and eat when you feel hungry.
fact: Your head loses about as much heat as any other bare part of the same size; the "most heat through your head" idea came from a test where only the head was left uncovered.
fact: The vitamin C idea was made famous by Linus Pauling, a great chemist with two Nobel Prizes, who was wrong about this outside his own work: for most people, taking lots of vitamin C does not stop colds; studies suggest it may shorten them slightly for some people.
fact: Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do not help a cold; taking them when not needed helps germs become resistant to them.
fact: Green or yellow snot is common in an ordinary cold; its colour alone does not show you need antibiotics.
fact: Piling on blankets to "sweat out" a fever can make you overheat; light clothes and plenty of drinks are better.
fact: Chicken soup does not cure a cold, but warm drinks and soup can soothe a sore throat and help you drink enough.
fact: Washing your hands well is one of the best ways to stop cold viruses spreading.
fact: An ill person should see a doctor if they stay ill a long time or get worse, whatever colour their snot is.
exercise: Nan at the door: "Put your coat on or you'll catch a cold!" What really causes a cold? | A virus caught from another person | Cold air getting deep into your chest | Going outside in winter without a coat | Breathing in frosty air much too fast
exercise: Why are colds more common in winter? | People crowd indoors and some viruses last longer in cold, dry air | The cold weather turns into a cold inside you | Winter air is full of fresh new viruses made by frost | People's bodies switch off their defences in December
exercise: Mum: "Don't go out with wet hair, you'll get ill!" What is true? | Wet hair can make you feel cold, but it can't give you a virus | Wet hair lets germs soak straight into your head through the skin | Wet hair turns into a cold within an hour | Wet hair is safe only in summer
exercise: Grandad says "Feed a cold, starve a fever!" What do doctors actually say? | Drink plenty and eat when hungry with either one | Eat as much food as you possibly can when you have a cold | Eat nothing at all for as long as you have a fever | Only drink ice-cold water with both, and eat nothing
exercise: Coach: "You lose most of your heat through your head!" Where did that idea come from? | An old test where people were wrapped up except their heads | A study showing heads are made of thinner skin | Doctors measuring that the brain is the hottest organ in the body | An advert from a hat company in the 1950s
exercise: Does the head lose more heat than other body parts? | No, about the same as any bare part of that size | Yes, about ninety per cent of all the heat you lose | Yes, because your hair lets heat escape much faster | No, the head loses no heat at all because of the skull
exercise: Auntie with her fizzy tablets: "Loads of vitamin C and you'll never get a cold." What do the big studies show? | It doesn't stop colds for most people, maybe shortens some | It stops every single cold if you take enough of it each day | It makes colds last twice as long | It only works if you take it at night
exercise: Why won't antibiotics help a cold? | Colds are viruses, and antibiotics kill bacteria | Colds are too small for medicine to reach | Antibiotics only ever work on grown-ups, not on children | Antibiotics need to be taken for a whole year
exercise: What can happen if people take antibiotics they don't need? | Germs can become resistant, so antibiotics work less well | The cold turns into a much worse flu | Their body stops making new blood | Nothing at all, extra is always safe
exercise: Jess blows her nose: "Green snot means a bad infection, I need medicine!" Is she right? | No, green or yellow snot is common in an ordinary cold | Yes, green snot always means you need antibiotics from the doctor | Yes, green snot is a sign of food poisoning | No, green snot means the cold is already over
exercise: Big brother wants to "sweat out" your fever under ten blankets. What's better? | Light covers and lots of drinks | Even more blankets and a hot-water bottle | A cold bath full of ice cubes | No drinks until the fever breaks
exercise: Gran brings chicken soup "to cure your cold". What is true? | It won't cure it, but warm soup can soothe a sore throat and help you drink | It kills the virus within a day | It makes the cold worse because of the salt | It only works if the chicken is free-range
exercise: Ollie jokes you catch a cold from a cold radiator. Where do cold viruses really come from? | Other people's coughs, sneezes and hands | Cold metal surfaces like railings in winter | Chilly draughts that come in under the door | Ice cubes floating in your cold drink at lunch
exercise: Which is one of the best ways to stop a cold spreading round your class? | Washing your hands well | Wearing a hat indoors | Keeping your hair dry | Taking antibiotics every week
exercise: Granny: "Don't go out in the rain without a hat, you'll catch your death!" When is she right that it's dangerous? | When it is very cold, getting soaked can chill you dangerously | Whenever it rains, because every raindrop carries cold viruses | Only when the rain is warm | Never, rain can't harm anybody at all
exercise: When should someone with a cold see a doctor? | If they stay ill a long time or get worse | As soon as their snot turns green or yellow | The very moment they sneeze two times in a row | Never, because colds can't ever get serious at all
test: Your mate goes to the South Pole station where nobody has a cold for months. A scientist arrives with a cold. What would happen? | People might start catching colds, because the virus has arrived | Everyone already had colds from the freezing air | Nobody could ever catch a cold that cold | The cold air would kill the virus at once
test: "Mum literally said if I sit by the open window I'll catch a cold. Is that a thing?" | Feeling chilly won't give you one, a virus from someone will | Yes, cold draughts carry colds in from outside through the gaps | Only if the window faces north | Yes, but only after dark
test: Two friends argue. Kai: "Colds come from viruses." Bea: "No, from cold weather, that's why it's called a cold!" Who is right? | Kai, the name is old and the cause is a virus | Bea, the name proves where it comes from | Both, half of colds are viruses and half weather | Neither, colds come from eating ice cream
test: Nan won't believe you about viruses and keeps nagging about your coat. What's the wisest move? | Wear the coat, it's cosy, and gently say the cold itself is a virus | Refuse to wear it until she admits she's wrong | Shout that she's been wrong for fifty years | Go out without it to prove your point
test: "doc gave me nothing for my cold!! rubbish doctor innit, I wanted antibiotics" What's the truth? | The doctor was right, antibiotics don't work on cold viruses | The doctor just forgot that antibiotics cure colds really fast | Antibiotics work on colds only in winter | He should have given a double dose
test: A cartoon shows a boy with a bare head on a ski slope, but wrapped up everywhere else, losing loads of heat from his head. What makes that true in the cartoon? | His head is the only bare part, so heat escapes there | Heads always lose most of your heat, whatever else you wear | Snow pulls heat out of hair faster | Ski slopes are colder at head height
test: "Me nan swears by chicken soup. Should I tell her it's a myth?" Best answer? | Just thank her, soup soothes you and kindness needn't be corrected | Yes, tell her straight away it's useless | Pour it away so she learns | Tell her only vitamin tablets work
test: What would happen if you never washed your hands all winter? | You'd be more likely to pass on and catch cold viruses | Nothing, because colds only ever come from breathing cold air | You'd be safer, because a bit of dirt protects you from germs | You'd only get colds in the summer instead of the winter
test: Your big brother says, "Feed a cold, starve a fever, it's like a rule of medicine." What do you say? | It's an old saying; with both you should drink and eat when hungry | He's right, and doctors still teach it to this day exactly as he says | It's the other way round: starve a cold | It only works for grown-ups
test: Why is the vitamin C story a good lesson about famous people? | Even a famous scientist can be wrong outside his own work | Famous scientists are never wrong about anything at all, ever | Vitamins were invented by a famous scientist | Famous people always test their ideas on themselves
test: A girl is shivering, soaking wet, on a freezing winter walk. Why should she get warm and dry fast? | Being soaked in great cold can lead to dangerous hypothermia | Her wet coat is full of cold viruses | Wet clothes make your snot turn green | She'll catch a cold within ten minutes if she doesn't change at once
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of the common cold? | It is caused by viruses transmitted between persons | It is caused by exposure of the body to low temperatures | It is caused by going out of doors with damp hair | It is caused by insufficient consumption of vitamin C
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antibiotic medicines are not prescribed for the common cold. | Antibiotics act upon bacteria, whereas the cold is viral | Antibiotics are reserved exclusively for adults | Antibiotics act only upon infections of the head | Antibiotics are ineffective during the winter months
exam: It is given that a febrile child is covered with numerous heavy blankets in order to "sweat out" the fever. Select the response consistent with established medical advice. | Light coverings and adequate fluids are to be preferred | Additional blankets are to be applied without delay to induce sweating | All fluids are to be withheld until the fever subsides | The child is to be immersed in iced water
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning loss of heat through the head. | The head loses heat in proportion to its exposed surface, as other parts do | The head is responsible for the greater part of all heat lost | The head loses no heat whatsoever when uncovered | Heat is lost through the head only during sleep
